CASE = [
 Q("<b>Case study.</b> In 1905 Lord Curzon announced the Partition of Bengal into two provinces. People in Calcutta, Dhaka and other towns protested. They burnt foreign cloth, boycotted British goods and used goods made in India. Leaders such as Lala Lajpat Rai, Bal Gangadhar Tilak and Bipin Chandra Pal supported the movement. (i) Who announced the partition? (ii) What does Swadeshi mean? (iii) Name the leaders known as Lal-Bal-Pal. (iv) Why did the partition rouse such strong opposition?",
   "(i) Lord Curzon (1). (ii) Of one's own country; use of Indian goods (1). (iii) Lala Lajpat Rai, Bal Gangadhar Tilak and Bipin Chandra Pal (1). (iv) It was seen as a British attempt to divide Bengal and weaken the growing national feeling (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q("<b>Case study.</b> Mahatma Gandhi returned to India from South Africa in 1915, where he had developed satyagraha. In 1930 he began the Salt March from Sabarmati Ashram and reached the coastal village of Dandi to break the salt law. The Congress had declared Purna Swaraj at Lahore in 1929. (i) In which year did Gandhi return to India? (ii) On what principles is satyagraha based? (iii) Where did the Salt March end? (iv) Why was salt chosen as a symbol of protest?",
   "(i) 1915 (1). (ii) Truth and non-violence (1). (iii) Dandi (1). (iv) Salt was used by everyone, and the tax on it burdened the poor, so it united people of all sections (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q("<b>Case study.</b> In 1899-1900 the Munda tribals of Chota Nagpur rose against outside landlords and the British in the Ulgulaan, or Great Tumult, led by Birsa Munda. In Punjab, the Kuka Movement under Baba Ram Singh rejected foreign goods and British rule. (i) Who led the Ulgulaan? (ii) In which region did it take place? (iii) Who led the Kuka Movement? (iv) Why are such tribal and peasant movements counted as part of the freedom struggle?",
   "(i) Birsa Munda (1). (ii) Chota Nagpur (1). (iii) Baba Ram Singh (1). (iv) They challenged British authority and exploitation and showed resistance among ordinary people, not only among educated leaders (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
]

